/// The one lock every test in this crate takes before touching the process
/// environment.
///
/// It has to be **one** lock for the whole crate, not one per module. `auth.rs`
/// and `lib.rs` compile into a single test binary and each used to keep its own
/// mutex — which reads like serialisation and is not, because two mutexes do
/// not exclude each other. They also never collided on a *value*: `auth.rs`
/// sets `SEANCE_JWT_SECRET` and friends, `lib.rs` sets `SEANCE_EVIDENCE_ROOTS`.
/// That is exactly what makes the bug easy to miss and hard to reproduce.
///
/// The race is not about the values. `setenv(3)` may reallocate the `environ`
/// array, so a `set_var` on any variable is a data race against a `getenv` for
/// any *other* variable anywhere in the process — which is why Rust made
/// `std::env::set_var` `unsafe` in edition 2024. `seance_core`'s corpus gate
/// already states the rule this crate was breaking: "a test that mutates shared
/// environment is a race against every other test in the binary anyway"
/// (`seance_core::test_support::corpus::resolve`). It avoids the problem by
/// passing the environment in; the tests here genuinely exercise `from_env`
/// paths and cannot, so they serialise instead.
///
/// Poisoning is recovered rather than propagated: a test that panicked while
/// holding this lock has already failed and reported, and turning that into a
/// cascade of unrelated failures in every later env test would bury it.
#[cfg(test)]
pub(crate) static TEST_ENV_LOCK: std::sync::Mutex<()> = std::sync::Mutex::new(());

